WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.001 --> 00:00:03.302
First, he's a third-term
US rep from Texas's 16th,

00:00:03.302 --> 00:00:06.106
who's the Democratic nominee
for Senate against Ted Cruz,

00:00:06.339 --> 00:00:08.474
Congressman Beto O'Rourke.

00:00:08.707 --> 00:00:10.009
(CHEERING AND APPLAUSE)

00:00:10.076 --> 00:00:11.244
BILL: Hey!

00:00:12.278 --> 00:00:14.647
How are you sir?
Nice to meet you.

00:00:15.281 --> 00:00:16.616
Wow. Glad to have you.

00:00:18.017 --> 00:00:19.953
Okay. Well, wow, that's--

00:00:20.220 --> 00:00:22.589
Look at that. They, uh...
Did you...

00:00:24.190 --> 00:00:26.659
Did you stack the audience here
with people--

00:00:26.792 --> 00:00:27.993
-No? Well they--
-No.

00:00:28.027 --> 00:00:29.429
They seem to know who you are
already.

00:00:29.495 --> 00:00:31.097
It's like when the Beatles
came to America.

00:00:31.163 --> 00:00:32.899
They didn't know
everybody would know them.

00:00:33.099 --> 00:00:34.333
Uh, so, for--

00:00:34.433 --> 00:00:36.435
Well, we're just learning
a lot of people,

00:00:36.502 --> 00:00:38.170
so first of all,
explain the name.

00:00:38.704 --> 00:00:42.608
So, born Robert Francis O'Rourke
in El Paso Texas,

00:00:42.642 --> 00:00:43.443
fourth generation.

00:00:43.710 --> 00:00:46.246
But like almost anyone born
Robert in El Paso,

00:00:46.312 --> 00:00:48.447
-called Beto from day one.
-BILL: I see.

00:00:48.547 --> 00:00:51.284
It's a nickname for Beto,
or, uh, for Robert or Alberto

00:00:51.317 --> 00:00:53.886
-or any name ending in -berto.
-BILL: You're full Irish?

00:00:54.286 --> 00:00:56.355
Um, pretty Irish.
Uh, especially tomorrow.

00:00:56.589 --> 00:00:58.725
-Yeah. (CHUCKLES)
-(AUDIENCE LAUGHTER)

00:00:59.158 --> 00:01:01.528
Okay. And you're running
against Ted Cruz.

00:01:02.061 --> 00:01:04.163
Okay-- Yes. Okay. So...

00:01:04.230 --> 00:01:06.766
(AUDIENCE CHEERS)

00:01:07.100 --> 00:01:09.335
Ted Cruz, we know,
is very disliked.

00:01:09.368 --> 00:01:10.402
Let me read some quotes.

00:01:10.636 --> 00:01:13.473
Uh, Al Franken said:
"Like Ted-- I like Ted Cruz

00:01:13.539 --> 00:01:15.341
more than most of my
other colleagues

00:01:15.441 --> 00:01:17.543
like Ted Cruz,
and I hate Ted Cruz.

00:01:17.810 --> 00:01:19.111
(AUDIENCE LAUGHTER)

00:01:19.478 --> 00:01:22.214
Bob Dole in 2015 said,
"There are a lot
of good candidates.

00:01:22.247 --> 00:01:24.250
I like nearly all of them,
except Cruz."

00:01:24.350 --> 00:01:25.351
BETO: Right. (LAUGHS)

00:01:25.418 --> 00:01:26.886
John Boehner:
"Lucifer in the flesh.

00:01:26.953 --> 00:01:29.889
Never worked with
a more miserable son of a bitch
in my life."

00:01:30.022 --> 00:01:31.758
(AUDIENCE LAUGHTER)

00:01:31.791 --> 00:01:34.927
Lindsey Graham:
"If you killed Ted Cruz
on the floor of the Senate

00:01:35.027 --> 00:01:36.262
nobody would convict you."

00:01:36.495 --> 00:01:38.398
-(AUDIENCE LAUGHTER)
-Uh, I just...

00:01:40.566 --> 00:01:42.635
My quest-- (CHUCKLES)
My question is,

00:01:43.236 --> 00:01:45.704
how bad are you gonna feel
if you lose to that guy?

00:01:45.838 --> 00:01:47.773
Yeah. Yeah. I-- I'll tell ya.

00:01:47.907 --> 00:01:50.943
There's something though
happening in Texas right now.

00:01:51.043 --> 00:01:55.281 line:0%
I've traveled to 226
of the 254 counties.

00:01:55.581 --> 00:01:56.982 line:0%
-BILL: Wow.
-People are so fired up,

00:01:57.082 --> 00:01:58.984 line:0%
regardless of party
or geography.

00:01:59.051 --> 00:02:00.886
So it's Democrats coming out
for sure.

00:02:01.153 --> 00:02:02.922
But it's also Republicans
and Independents.

00:02:02.955 --> 00:02:05.758
It's in the really big cities.
It's in the real small towns.

00:02:05.925 --> 00:02:09.128
The places that no-one
has ever visited,
including Ted Cruz,

00:02:09.362 --> 00:02:12.131
because for the first four years
he was in the Senate,

00:02:12.264 --> 00:02:13.433
he was actually in Iowa,

00:02:13.533 --> 00:02:16.035
and New Hampshire
and South Carolina and Nevada.

00:02:16.135 --> 00:02:17.503
He visited all 99 counties

00:02:17.536 --> 00:02:18.971
-of Iowa.
-BILL: Of Iowa, right.

00:02:19.138 --> 00:02:22.441
Uh, and so there are places
where they haven't seen
a US senator,

00:02:22.675 --> 00:02:25.178
and so when we show up,
hold a town-hall meeting,

00:02:25.244 --> 00:02:27.179
listen to the people
that we wanna serve

00:02:27.246 --> 00:02:29.015
and represent, we're really
making a connection.

00:02:29.082 --> 00:02:31.784
And folks
are committing themselves
to the work ahead,

00:02:31.818 --> 00:02:34.386
and I think we've got a great
shot at winning in November.

00:02:34.820 --> 00:02:35.822
(AUDIENCE CHEERING)

00:02:35.855 --> 00:02:36.922
Well, we, uh...

00:02:37.156 --> 00:02:38.924
We've heard that before
about elections.

00:02:38.991 --> 00:02:40.459
-I mean, we--
-Yeah, absolutely.

00:02:40.559 --> 00:02:43.096
I always wonder why Texas
and California,

00:02:43.129 --> 00:02:45.264
have very similar
Latino demographics.

00:02:45.464 --> 00:02:50.636
I think the eligible Latino
voting number here
in California is 28 percent.

00:02:50.703 --> 00:02:51.704
Same as Texas.

00:02:52.104 --> 00:02:54.841
Why are we so Blue
and Texas is not Blue?

00:02:55.008 --> 00:02:58.010
It's only 43 percent white now,
Texas. Is that right?

00:02:58.077 --> 00:02:59.011
That's right.

00:02:59.545 --> 00:03:02.248
Texas is one of the most
gerrymandered states
in the union.

00:03:02.448 --> 00:03:04.450
It is also a non-voting state.

00:03:04.483 --> 00:03:06.185
And those two things
are connected.

00:03:06.218 --> 00:03:07.453
-There are some people--
-BILL: Non-voting?

00:03:07.587 --> 00:03:10.489
That's right. We're 49th
in the country in voter turnout.

00:03:10.856 --> 00:03:14.126
The courts four times alone
last year found that

00:03:14.160 --> 00:03:17.630
our gerrymandering is in part
based on race and ethnicity.

00:03:17.697 --> 00:03:19.999
There are some people
who are not supposed to vote.

00:03:20.033 --> 00:03:23.268
There are some people's voices
that are not supposed
to be heard.

00:03:23.435 --> 00:03:26.673
It's on us to run a campaign
that brings in everyone,

00:03:26.739 --> 00:03:27.573
from every community,

00:03:27.607 --> 00:03:29.809
every community
within every community,

00:03:30.009 --> 00:03:30.943
into this campaign.

00:03:31.010 --> 00:03:32.178
To make sure that they're heard,

00:03:32.345 --> 00:03:34.013
and that they're actually
leading what we're doing.

00:03:34.079 --> 00:03:34.981
I think when we do that,

00:03:35.214 --> 00:03:36.549
we're gonna give people
a reason to vote.

00:03:36.782 --> 00:03:40.886
And we saw the greatest
Democratic primary
midterm Senate turnout

00:03:40.953 --> 00:03:43.356
last Tuesday, in 36 years.

00:03:43.689 --> 00:03:45.892
-Uh, we saw-- Yeah.
-(AUDIENCE APPLAUSE)

00:03:47.526 --> 00:03:51.630
-So, votes are--
-The Republicans came out
in Texas too,

00:03:51.697 --> 00:03:53.466
-in bigger numbers.
-They sure did. Y'know what?

00:03:53.499 --> 00:03:55.735
We don't have-- This is not
in the bag by any means.

00:03:55.802 --> 00:03:58.638
Not at all. And you know what?
That's a great thing,

00:03:58.705 --> 00:04:02.374
because folks from both parties,
Independents as well,

00:04:02.608 --> 00:04:05.110
are getting off the sidelines
and into the game.

00:04:05.244 --> 00:04:06.979
But they recognize
that this year,

00:04:07.312 --> 00:04:08.914
everything that they care about,
um,

00:04:08.948 --> 00:04:11.551
everything that they've told
their kids about this country,

00:04:11.951 --> 00:04:13.052
is on the line.

00:04:13.286 --> 00:04:15.688
And for Amy and me,
that's what motivated us.

00:04:15.755 --> 00:04:17.656
We anticipate the question
our kids,

00:04:17.857 --> 00:04:20.259
Ulysses and Molly and Henry,
are gonna ask us:

00:04:20.526 --> 00:04:22.628
"When you had the chance
to do something in 2018

00:04:22.661 --> 00:04:25.397
when they were talking
about walls and Muslim bans

00:04:25.431 --> 00:04:27.333
and the press
as the enemy of the people,

00:04:27.466 --> 00:04:28.534
what did you guys do?"

00:04:28.934 --> 00:04:32.772
And we're gonna tell them that
we and the 28-million of Texas

00:04:33.005 --> 00:04:35.774
helped this country
get back on track
and on the right direction, so--

00:04:35.807 --> 00:04:38.611
-(AUDIENCE CHEERING)
-Um, I feel lucky
to be a part of it.

00:04:40.012 --> 00:04:40.980
BILL: Um...

00:04:42.081 --> 00:04:43.282
And you-- And you don't--

00:04:43.616 --> 00:04:45.084
And you don't take PAC money?

00:04:45.451 --> 00:04:47.286
I don't take a dime
of PAC money.

00:04:47.420 --> 00:04:49.322
No corporations.
No special interests.

00:04:49.455 --> 00:04:50.956
(AUDIENCE CHEERING)

00:04:50.989 --> 00:04:51.824
BILL: For those, uh...

00:04:52.325 --> 00:04:54.326
For anyone who's watching
and might not be
a political junkie,

00:04:54.393 --> 00:04:55.461
what is PAC money?

00:04:55.794 --> 00:04:58.364
-What does that mean, PAC?
-So, political action committees

00:04:58.597 --> 00:05:01.300 line:0%
um, represent the corporations
and interests

00:05:01.434 --> 00:05:02.635 line:0%
that have business
before Congress.

00:05:02.735 --> 00:05:04.704 line:0%
So, uh, the pharmaceutical
industries,

00:05:04.737 --> 00:05:07.373 line:0%
the telecom industries,
the energy industries,

00:05:07.707 --> 00:05:09.575 line:0%
um, the insurance industries.

00:05:09.809 --> 00:05:11.611 line:0%
And they give money
to members of Congress,

00:05:11.711 --> 00:05:14.380
not just for access,
although that's part of it.

00:05:14.580 --> 00:05:18.818
Um, they're also buying outcomes
and actual legislative language

00:05:18.984 --> 00:05:22.355
that appears in the bills
and the bills that become laws.

00:05:22.421 --> 00:05:25.491
When you wonder why
Congress is so dysfunctional,

00:05:25.591 --> 00:05:27.492
why it doesn't represent
the interests

00:05:27.793 --> 00:05:29.562
of the people it purports
to serve,

00:05:29.895 --> 00:05:33.065
it's because it's so tied
to the sources of money
that are coming in,

00:05:33.131 --> 00:05:34.467
and it's those corporations.

00:05:34.767 --> 00:05:37.303
It's why pharmaceutical prices
continue to rise.

00:05:37.503 --> 00:05:40.873
It's why, uh,
we're the least insured country

00:05:40.939 --> 00:05:41.840
in the developed world.

00:05:42.140 --> 00:05:44.777
It's why we're missing out
on so many great opportunities

00:05:45.011 --> 00:05:47.980
to do the big, important,
ambitious work before us.

00:05:48.180 --> 00:05:49.515
And for Democrats especially,

00:05:49.649 --> 00:05:51.550
it's not enough to decry
Citizens United,

00:05:51.584 --> 00:05:55.287
and say that all this big money
sloshing through the halls
of Congress is bad.

00:05:55.521 --> 00:05:57.723
We've got to start
walking the talk.

00:05:57.790 --> 00:06:00.692
So in this campaign,
we have out-raised Ted Cruz

00:06:00.893 --> 00:06:02.494
by well more than
a million dollars

00:06:02.561 --> 00:06:04.029
without taking a dime from PACs.

00:06:04.130 --> 00:06:06.566
-All people. Human beings.
-(AUDIENCE CHEERING)

00:06:09.868 --> 00:06:11.871
But don't forget
he's a giant asshole.

00:06:11.971 --> 00:06:12.972
That-- That's true.

00:06:13.873 --> 00:06:15.841
Vote so we'll never have
to wonder

00:06:15.907 --> 00:06:17.976
-who it is I represent...
-BILL: Right.

00:06:18.043 --> 00:06:18.744
...or who I'm voting for.

00:06:19.144 --> 00:06:20.780
It's gonna be the people
of Texas every single time.

00:06:20.813 --> 00:06:23.015
So, what-- What would you say
are your top three?

00:06:23.115 --> 00:06:26.552
If you had to say your top three
most important issues
facing this country.

00:06:26.619 --> 00:06:27.720
-Yeah.
-What are yours?

00:06:28.153 --> 00:06:32.224
Too many people can't find a job
or are in a job right now

00:06:32.291 --> 00:06:33.759
that does not pay a living wage.

00:06:34.126 --> 00:06:36.929
And they need the dignity
and the function and purpose

00:06:36.962 --> 00:06:38.798
-that comes with work.
-BILL: Number one is jobs.

00:06:38.964 --> 00:06:41.466
Number two: If you're not
healthy enough to go to that job

00:06:41.533 --> 00:06:43.869
or finish your education
or raise your family,

00:06:43.936 --> 00:06:45.338
you're not doing yourself
any good.

00:06:45.404 --> 00:06:46.638
You're not doing the rest of us
any good.

00:06:46.705 --> 00:06:48.808
So, universal healthcare.
The ability for everyone

00:06:48.841 --> 00:06:50.942
-to see a doctor, regardless...
-(AUDIENCE CHEERING)

00:06:51.009 --> 00:06:52.211
of income.

00:06:52.545 --> 00:06:54.813
So you're for... single-payer?

00:06:55.080 --> 00:06:57.182
If that's how we get there,
uh, that's how we get there.

00:06:57.215 --> 00:06:59.886
-I'm-- I'm open to--
-Whoa, that's a big "if,"
Congressman.

00:06:59.952 --> 00:07:02.455
"If that's how we get there."
That's a big issue to go,
"Well, if--"

00:07:02.522 --> 00:07:04.657
Listen, I-- I think Medicare
works really well.

00:07:04.690 --> 00:07:06.225
Its overhead cost
is two percent.

00:07:06.258 --> 00:07:08.494
You compare that against
private insurance companies,

00:07:08.627 --> 00:07:10.329
sixteen, seventeen percent.

00:07:10.429 --> 00:07:13.499
It's effective, it's efficient.
I think that's a great model.

00:07:13.632 --> 00:07:15.801
So you're where Bernie Sanders
is with healthcare?

00:07:15.901 --> 00:07:18.337
Yeah, I think he's got
a good plan,

00:07:18.437 --> 00:07:20.439
and I would support his bill
in the Senate.

00:07:20.506 --> 00:07:22.108
-(AUDIENCE MEMBER WHISTLES)
-I think--

00:07:22.308 --> 00:07:23.943
(AUDIENCE CHEERING)

00:07:24.076 --> 00:07:25.077
Uh... And I'll tell you...

00:07:25.110 --> 00:07:28.080
I'll tell you another issue
where Texas

00:07:28.281 --> 00:07:29.982
I think, is uniquely positioned
to lead.

00:07:30.182 --> 00:07:32.584
We are the most diverse state
in the country.

00:07:32.718 --> 00:07:35.687
I'd like to think we are the
defining immigrant experience.

00:07:35.721 --> 00:07:37.590
The city I represent,
El Paso, Texas,

00:07:37.957 --> 00:07:40.860
is one of, if not the,
safest cities in America.

00:07:41.027 --> 00:07:42.628
And a quarter of those
that I represent

00:07:42.661 --> 00:07:44.697
were born in another country.
They chose us.

00:07:44.897 --> 00:07:47.900
They're making us stronger,
safer, more secure.

00:07:48.133 --> 00:07:51.604
We should be the ones
leading the fight
for immigration reform,

00:07:51.637 --> 00:07:53.772
rewriting immigration law
in our own image,

00:07:53.939 --> 00:07:56.909
from our own traditions
and values and interests.

00:07:57.176 --> 00:08:00.413
Um, Ted Cruz was
the sole senator

00:08:00.580 --> 00:08:02.881
out of 98 who showed up
to work that day a month ago,

00:08:03.182 --> 00:08:05.951 line:0%
the sole senator to vote no
on just proceeding

00:08:06.085 --> 00:08:08.787 line:0%
on debate and discussion
to be able to protect Dreamers

00:08:08.920 --> 00:08:11.457 line:0%
and allow those
more than one-million
of our fellow Americans

00:08:11.524 --> 00:08:13.426 line:0%
to contribute to their
full potential,

00:08:13.526 --> 00:08:14.926 line:0%
to the success of this country.

00:08:14.993 --> 00:08:17.129
We need a senator
who leads on those issues.

00:08:17.496 --> 00:08:19.799
-BILL: Okay, so--
-(CHEERING AND APPLAUSE)

00:08:19.899 --> 00:08:22.534
So jobs is your one,
healthcare is your two.

00:08:22.601 --> 00:08:24.069
-What was your three?
-Immigration.

00:08:24.136 --> 00:08:24.904
Immigration. Wow.

00:08:25.371 --> 00:08:27.539
So Russia, the environment,
they don't make the top three.

00:08:27.873 --> 00:08:29.608
Russia and the environment
are really important.

00:08:29.641 --> 00:08:31.476
-I mean, here's another--
-BILL: Four, five?

00:08:31.577 --> 00:08:32.778
-Yeah. There you go.
-(GROANS)

00:08:32.944 --> 00:08:35.280
Here's one where Texas
can lead. Listen.

00:08:35.914 --> 00:08:38.717
You know us as an energy state,
right? And we're proud of that.

00:08:38.784 --> 00:08:41.620
Lot of good jobs
in the oil and gas industry,

00:08:41.653 --> 00:08:42.687
the refining industry.

00:08:43.021 --> 00:08:44.890
But we're also leading
the country right now

00:08:44.924 --> 00:08:46.191
in the generation of wind power.

00:08:46.491 --> 00:08:48.927
We will soon lead the country
in the generation
of solar power.

00:08:48.961 --> 00:08:51.831
There are more clean energy jobs
in the state of Texas today

00:08:51.897 --> 00:08:54.667
than there are carbon energy
jobs in the state of Texas.

00:08:54.734 --> 00:08:56.769
And it's not even close.
So whether it is

00:08:56.936 --> 00:08:59.171
saving the planet for
the generations that follow us,

00:08:59.405 --> 00:09:02.942
recognizing that maybe
man-made climate change
didn't cause Harvey,

00:09:03.108 --> 00:09:04.943
but man-made climate change
will ensure

00:09:04.976 --> 00:09:07.313
that there are more Harveys,
that they are more frequent,

00:09:07.346 --> 00:09:09.949
they are more intense,
there are more droughts
in the Panhandle.

00:09:10.016 --> 00:09:12.617
We have less opportunities
to grow our own food

00:09:12.684 --> 00:09:14.887
and pass this planet on
to the next generation.

00:09:14.954 --> 00:09:16.988
Whether it's jobs,
whether it's economic growth,

00:09:17.088 --> 00:09:18.457
for all the right reasons
in the world,

00:09:18.724 --> 00:09:22.461
Texas can and should lead
on environmental issues
for this country.

00:09:22.662 --> 00:09:24.697
-And-- And, uh...
-(APPLAUSE)

00:09:26.965 --> 00:09:29.735
And what about, uh, weed?
I ask for a friend.

00:09:29.935 --> 00:09:31.771
-BETO: Yeah. (LAUGHS)
-(AUDIENCE LAUGHTER)

00:09:32.371 --> 00:09:33.338
Um...

00:09:34.039 --> 00:09:36.408
No, but-- but-- I mean--
Like, I love Texas.

00:09:36.542 --> 00:09:39.845
I-- Texas knows how to party.
I've gone there as a guest.

00:09:39.945 --> 00:09:42.615
Y'know, as a comedian,
for, like, 35 years.

00:09:42.681 --> 00:09:45.318
-You've come to El Paso.
-Many times. I love El Paso.

00:09:45.384 --> 00:09:47.219
San Antonio, Austin, Dallas,
Houston.

00:09:47.252 --> 00:09:49.721
They're all great cities.
They're all fun places.

00:09:49.988 --> 00:09:52.390
And they love their weed.
Uh...

00:09:52.991 --> 00:09:54.893
California finally
took the plunge.

00:09:54.993 --> 00:09:56.762
Texas seems like
the kind of place

00:09:56.862 --> 00:09:59.298
where it's a tough--
tough road to hoe there.

00:09:59.698 --> 00:10:01.133
Where are you on that one?

00:10:01.333 --> 00:10:02.635
I've joined a bill,

00:10:02.735 --> 00:10:04.135
written by
a Republican colleague,

00:10:04.236 --> 00:10:06.806
former prosecutor
who waged the war on drugs,

00:10:06.939 --> 00:10:08.974
saw how futile it was
from the front lines,

00:10:09.274 --> 00:10:11.944
to end the federal prohibition
on marijuana.

00:10:12.144 --> 00:10:12.945
-(APPLAUSE)
-Um...

00:10:13.078 --> 00:10:15.280
So legal in Texas
is where we're going?

00:10:15.347 --> 00:10:16.048
It should be.

00:10:16.615 --> 00:10:18.784
-And we should control
and regulate its sale and...
-Yes.

00:10:18.951 --> 00:10:20.853
-Uh, listen. We--
-It unites people.

00:10:20.953 --> 00:10:22.488
-We have the world's--
-Right? No, no.

00:10:22.554 --> 00:10:24.890
They smoke it in the city,
they smoke it in the country,

00:10:24.957 --> 00:10:27.026
the hippies smoke it,
the rednecks smoke it.

00:10:27.126 --> 00:10:29.462
It's-- It's--
It is a uniting thing.

00:10:29.929 --> 00:10:31.464
-And--
-I'll show you after the show.

00:10:31.497 --> 00:10:32.465
No, I'm cool.

00:10:33.032 --> 00:10:34.733
So, we get a chance
to do the right thing.

00:10:34.866 --> 00:10:38.003
We have the world's largest
prison population bar none.

00:10:38.036 --> 00:10:40.339
We have folks who are
wasting away behind bars...

00:10:40.406 --> 00:10:41.940
-BILL: Yes.
-...who need to be
raising families,

00:10:42.040 --> 00:10:44.643
uh, working, paying taxes,
starting jobs,

00:10:44.710 --> 00:10:46.411
touring in punk rock bands,
writing novels...

00:10:46.511 --> 00:10:48.747
-Whatever they're supposed to do
in life...
-BILL: Right. (LAUGHS)

00:10:48.780 --> 00:10:50.249
...let's allow them to do that,
not lock 'em up.

00:10:50.315 --> 00:10:52.551
All right. I'll be rooting
for ya, and I know they will.

00:10:52.618 --> 00:10:54.987
-All right, thank you...
-(AUDIENCE CHEERING)

00:10:55.688 --> 00:10:57.056
BILL: Thank you very much.

